IMPLEMENTATION CONTRACT


1. SHARED INTERACTION BOUNDARY

Extract only interaction behavior demonstrated by BOTH real Terminal consumers.

Place the shared interaction primitive in a neutral presentation/interaction
location that is legal under the current repository dependency boundaries.

Do not make one feature import another feature's internal UI merely to reuse
Terminal interaction behavior.

A location conceptually similar to:

src/apps/terminalInteraction/

is acceptable only if it fits the current accepted repository structure.

If current architecture provides a more appropriate shared UI/interaction
location, use that instead and report the final location.

Do not change repository dependency rules merely to accommodate this primitive.

Do not place reusable interaction ownership inside the NODE-specific Terminal
module merely because NODE currently has the more mature implementation.

The shared layer may own:

- input value
- input/output refs
- per-instance history
- history position
- preserved live draft
- dispatch guard
- IME/composition state
- follow-tail state
- narrow focus/scroll helpers

It must not own:

- GameState
- GameActions
- command parsing
- command registries
- filesystem semantics
- RemoteSession
- Process semantics
- NodeScan
- operating-system identity


Do not build a giant UniversalTerminal component.

Prefer a small hook plus pure helpers and the minimum honest shared styling.


2. BOTH TERMINALS MUST ACTUALLY CONSUME IT

Migrate both:

- NODE-OS Terminal
- RACK-OS Terminal

onto the accepted shared interaction foundation.

Do not leave NODE with a private duplicate implementation while only RACK uses
the new abstraction.

Keep adapter-specific output and command behavior local.


3. HISTORY + LIVE DRAFT

History is ephemeral per-Terminal-instance interaction state.

NODE history and RACK history remain separate.

Required behavior:

current draft:
analy

ArrowUp
→ older command

ArrowDown back to live position
→ analy


History restoration must:

- keep focus when already focused
- place the caret at the end after the value is rendered
- avoid select-all
- avoid scrollIntoView / page movement

`clear` clears visible output only.

It does not clear history.


4. COMMON INPUT BEHAVIOR

Both Terminal inputs must use terminal-appropriate browser behavior:

- autoCapitalize="none"
- autoComplete="off"
- autoCorrect="off"
- spellCheck={false}
- enterKeyHint="send"

Opening a Terminal must not auto-focus the input on mobile.


5. IME / COMPOSITION

Enter must not submit while text composition is active.

Use the current React/browser event model reliably; do not assume every Enter
means command submission.


6. ASYNC DISPATCH GUARD

The shared interaction layer must support synchronous or asynchronous command
adapters without owning command semantics.

While one guarded dispatch is pending:

new submit
→ ignored / guarded

Do NOT:

- disable the focused input
- queue another command
- run concurrent commands
- prevent the player from typing the next draft


Submitting captures the current command string, then clears only that submitted
draft.

Text typed afterward belongs to the next draft.

Late resolve/reject of the previous command must never overwrite or clear it.


7. GUARDED DISPATCH ALWAYS RECOVERS

Dispatch cleanup must run through `finally`.

A success or rejection must always release the interaction guard.

A command rejection must become controlled Terminal failure output rather than
an unhandled Promise rejection.

The shared interaction layer does not decide the exact OS-specific failure text.

The shared interaction layer may expose a narrow controlled dispatch-failure
signal/result to the Terminal adapter.

The adapter owns player-facing failure presentation.

Do not automatically render raw:

- Error stacks
- internal exception names
- arbitrary implementation messages

into Terminal output merely because a Promise rejected.

8. FOCUS OWNERSHIP

Normal submit should preserve an already-active editing session.

The input remains enabled and the keyboard should remain open.

Where immediate focus preservation is needed, use behavior equivalent to:

focus({ preventScroll: true })


Do NOT implement:

await command
finally
  input.focus()


Async completion must not steal focus after the player has:

- blurred the input
- dismissed the keyboard
- focused another control
- changed section/app
- disconnected
- unmounted the Terminal

Leaving or unmounting the Terminal does not by itself cancel the underlying
gameplay/application operation.

If an already-submitted command represents a real application operation, that
operation continues according to its existing semantics.

Only Terminal interaction/presentation behavior is abandoned when the surface
is no longer active.

A later completion must not:

- reopen the Terminal
- restore Terminal focus
- reopen the keyboard
- restore stale presentation state

Do not introduce command cancellation semantics in this Work Order.


9. ONE REAL OUTPUT SCROLLER

Each Terminal has exactly one actual vertical output scroller.

The element that owns:

overflow-y

must itself own:

data-editing-scroll-owner


Its geometry must support the equivalent of:

min-height: 0;
overflow-y: auto;
overflow-x: hidden;


Terminal scrolling must not chain into Shell/Page scrolling.

Prefer:

overscroll-behavior-y: contain

unless tested current-platform behavior requires stronger containment.

Do not attach the editing-scroll-owner marker to a decorative ancestor.


10. SHELL REMAINS VIEWPORT AUTHORITY

Do not modify the architecture established for mobile editing.

Do NOT introduce Terminal-owned:

- VisualViewport listeners
- keyboard-height state
- window.scrollTo fixes
- scrollIntoView keyboard fixes
- body transforms
- viewport polling
- fake keyboard dimensions

Shell remains the only external editing/viewport authority.


11. FOLLOW-TAIL

Replace unconditional NODE output autoscroll with shared follow-tail semantics.

Required transitions:

user is near bottom
→ followingTail = true

user manually scrolls away
→ followingTail = false

user manually returns near bottom
→ followingTail = true

player submits a command
→ followingTail = true

async output arrives while followingTail = false
→ remains false


A late async result must not yank a player away from old output they are
reading.

Programmatic scrolling performed by the Terminal itself to follow the tail must
not accidentally be interpreted as deliberate user scroll-away.

The implementation must preserve the semantic distinction:

Terminal follows newest output
→ remains followingTail

player deliberately scrolls away from newest output
→ followingTail = false


12. NEAR-BOTTOM + POST-RENDER SCROLL

Use a small pure helper to determine whether the user is near the bottom.

Use a modest tolerance in approximately the 24–32 CSS-pixel range rather than
exact equality.

When follow-tail is active:

new output renders
→ then scroll to newest rendered content


Do not scroll against stale pre-render `scrollHeight`.

Prefer a normal controlled React effect/layout-effect mechanism.

Do not add MutationObserver unless current accepted implementation demonstrates
a concrete need.


13. REMOVE FAKE SCAN PRESENTATION DURATION

Remove:

SCAN_MIN_DISPLAY_MS

or any equivalent artificial minimum presentation delay.

If Scan currently has no represented duration:

scan
→ actual result immediately


Do not replace it with another fake delay, animation, or timer.

Do not alter real Process timing.


14. OUTPUT ACCESSIBILITY

Do not make the entire continuously changing Terminal history an `aria-live`
region.

Normal output remains normal readable/selectable DOM content.

Targeted status announcements may remain where useful.

Do not broadly refactor TargetToken in this slice.

Preserve its:

- semantic button
- accessible naming
- keyboard activation
- direct user-triggered clipboard write
- pointer focus-preservation behavior
- local copy feedback


